<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Keep background merges out of the page cache.

  Railway bills cgroup memory, which includes page cache. This instance's
  ClickHouse process uses ~1.9-2.3 GB resident, yet the service bills 6-13 GB —
  the difference is page cache, and with no memory pressure the kernel never
  drops it. Capping the container to force eviction was tried twice (4 GB and
  8 GB) and both times collapsed query latency under concurrency, so the cache
  supply cannot be squeezed; the accumulation has to be prevented at the source.

  The dominant source is the merge pipeline: ingest runs 24/7, and background
  merges continuously rewrite parts, streaming GBs/day through the cache for
  data no query will read that way. Direct I/O keeps merge reads and writes out
  of the cache entirely. The default threshold only engages for merges over
  10 GiB — on a ~26 GB database that is effectively never. 128 MiB makes it the
  norm while leaving the smallest (hot, recent) merges cached.

  Query reads are deliberately untouched: the feed windowing already bounds
  what queries pull in, and their cache IS load-bearing (see the failed caps).

  max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool bounds how big a single merge can
  get (default 150 GB). The 12-13 GB memory spikes on the half-hour coincide
  with large merges; 8 GiB caps the working set of any one merge without
  meaningfully changing steady-state part counts at this data size.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <merge_tree>
        <min_merge_bytes_to_use_direct_io>134217728</min_merge_bytes_to_use_direct_io>
        <max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>8589934592</max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
